column3:
It's vulnerable because the user controls the content of $userinput, the variable name doesn't matter. Real code would look like this:<p><pre><code>  #!/usr/bin/perl

  print "Enter filename: ";
  $filename = <STDIN>;
  open(file_handler, "$filename");
</code></pre>
If you see this code, you know that you can execute any command by giving it at the filename prompt and appending a '|' character. For example giving <i>cat /etc/passwd |zenity --text-info |</i> will popup a dialog with the content of /etc/passwd.

column73:
True but perl makes it amazingly easy to exploit.<p>Suppose that you know the source contains this line:<p><pre><code>  open(file_handler, "$userinput");
</code></pre>
How much work and what assumptions do you need to exploit this? In perl it's that easy:<p><pre><code>  $userinput = "cat /etc/passwd |zenity --text-info |";
  open(file_handler, "$userinput");</code></pre>
